  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "This team doesn't fold when it matters.",
    "what_this_means": "Grisham supplied the power again. His second home run, his second two-RBI night in as many games. But the story was not about one player carrying a quiet lineup. The Yankees got nine hits. Ben Rice went 2-for-2. They had runners on in the first and sixth innings and converted nothing until the eighth, when they needed it most. That gap between opportunity and execution is supposed to break games open. Instead, they waited. They trailed after six full innings. Then they scored four in the eighth and didn't look back. Cam Schlittler held it together with 6.2 innings of two-run ball. A line that would have been wasted if the lineup couldn't find its moment. The bullpen closed clean. Three wins in a row. This is what happens when a team has enough depth to survive early silence and enough discipline to strike when it finally arrives.",
    "what_to_watch": "Cavalli starts for Washington tonight at 1:35 PM ET with a 3.88 ERA. Will Warren takes the ball for the Yankees in a rematch that matters more now. Three straight wins changes how you walk into a ballpark.",
